Dear Kamil,

Thank you for the clear and detailed introduction to CMLP.

Under our standard Flow Music commercial terms, a paid subscriber may use m=
usic generated through Flow Music in their own commercial projects. However=
, the standard license does not include the right to sublicense, resell, re=
distribute, or offer the generated music catalogue to third-party business =
customers under separate agreements.

Public-performance and collecting-society obligations may also vary dependi=
ng on the country, venue and specific use. Therefore, under our standard pl=
an, we cannot guarantee that fees to organizations such as ZAiKS, GEMA, PRS=
 or ASCAP would never apply.

For the B2B licensing model you described, we would need to discuss a separ=
ate written enterprise agreement covering:

=E2=80=A2 sublicensing rights;
=E2=80=A2 territories and license duration;
=E2=80=A2 customer and venue types;
=E2=80=A2 catalogue scope and expected usage;
=E2=80=A2 reporting and content-protection arrangements;
=E2=80=A2 allocation of public-performance and collecting-society obligatio=
ns.

We are open to reviewing this opportunity under a mutual NDA.

To help us evaluate it, could you please provide:

=E2=80=A2 your initial target countries;
=E2=80=A2 estimated number and types of business customers;
=E2=80=A2 expected catalogue size and monthly music volume;
=E2=80=A2 how the music would be delivered and tracked;
=E2=80=A2 your proposed commercial model and estimated budget.

Once we have this information, we can determine whether a custom worldwide =
or long-term license is feasible and arrange a short call.

Best regards,

